---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import FigureIsland from '../charts/FigureIsland'
import { FIGURES } from '../lib/figures'
import { loadRuns, loadManifest, latestEpoch } from '../lib/data'
import { rowsFor, packRows } from '../lib/measures'
import { buildGroups, fmt } from '../charts/engine'
import { armInfo, REPO_URL } from '../lib/roster'

const epoch = latestEpoch()
const manifest = loadManifest(epoch)
const runs = loadRuns(epoch)
const valid = runs.filter(r => r.validity_ok && r.score != null)
const dnf = runs.filter(r => !r.validity_ok).length
const arms = [...new Set(runs.map(r => r.arm))]
const models = [...new Set(runs.map(r => r.model))]
const verdicts = valid.reduce((a, r) => a + r.denominator, 0)

// Key results, computed from the same rows the figures use, at the site's
// default slice: ZeroShot + MultiTurn protocols, both batteries.
const ctxFig = FIGURES.find(f => f.slug === 'context-tokens')!
const accFig = FIGURES.find(f => f.slug === 'accuracy')!
const relFig = FIGURES.find(f => f.slug === 'pass-rate')!
const sel = { exams: ['ZeroShotExam', 'MultiTurnExam'], battery: 'both' as const, model: 'all' as const }
const ctxGroups = buildGroups(ctxFig, rowsFor(runs, 'ctx'), { ...sel, columns: 'per-model' }, false)
const accGroups = buildGroups(accFig, rowsFor(runs, 'pct'), { ...sel, columns: 'pooled' }, false)
const relGroups = buildGroups(relFig, rowsFor(runs, 'pct'), { ...sel, columns: 'pooled' }, false)
const ta = (gs: typeof accGroups) => gs.find(g => g.arm.role === 'trueArchitect')
const cold = (gs: typeof accGroups) => gs.find(g => g.arm.id === 'cold')
// per-model context comparison at each model both TA and CC Bare ran
const pairs = (ta(ctxGroups)?.columns ?? []).map(c => {
  const m = c.models[0]
  const cc = cold(ctxGroups)?.columns.find(x => x.models[0] === m)
  return cc && c.value != null && cc.value != null ? { model: c.label, ta: c.value, cc: cc.value, ratio: cc.value / c.value } : null
}).filter((x): x is NonNullable<typeof x> => !!x)
const ratioMin = pairs.length ? Math.min(...pairs.map(p => p.ratio)) : null
const ratioMax = pairs.length ? Math.max(...pairs.map(p => p.ratio)) : null
const accTA = ta(accGroups)?.pooled ?? null, accCC = cold(accGroups)?.pooled ?? null
const relTA = ta(relGroups)?.pooled ?? null, relCC = cold(relGroups)?.pooled ?? null
const bestIndexer = accGroups.filter(g => g.arm.role === 'indexer').sort((a, b) => (b.pooled ?? 0) - (a.pooled ?? 0))[0]
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '')
const repoKey = Object.keys(manifest.repositories)[0]
const repo = manifest.repositories[repoKey]
const tree = `${REPO_URL}/tree/main`, blob = `${REPO_URL}/blob/main`
---
<Base title="Summary">
  <div class="prose">
  <div class="kicker">{manifest.epoch.label} · public proof package</div>
  <h1>Does TrueArchitect's codebase index make an AI coding agent more accurate, more consistent and cheaper than lexical search?</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    This site reports one benchmark epoch in which the TrueArchitect <em>Ask</em> agent, the model plus TrueArchitect's index of the
    codebase, built by its own proprietary indexing system and designed for LLM consumption, answered {repo.batteries.reduce((a, b) => a + b.questions, 0)} questions
    about <a href={repo.source_url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{repo.source_url.replace('https://github.com/', '')}</a> under three exam
    protocols, beside bare Claude Code, bare Codex, bare Cursor Agent, and five codebase-indexing tools installed on Claude Code,
    at the same models and from the same clean checkout. Every figure is computed from the run records in the
    public repository, and every number links to the runs behind it.
  </p>

  <h2>What is measured</h2>
  <p>
    The benchmark measures <b>codebase understanding</b>: whether an agent, asked a question about a codebase it has never seen, arrives at the
    correct answer. Understanding precedes development. Correct code follows from a correct reading of the existing code and from loading the
    right context; an agent that misreads the codebase writes against a wrong model of it. No protocol in this epoch asks the agent to write
    code; every question tests context-building alone. Protocols in development extend the same method to planning code changes
    (refactors and new features) and to executing plans.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <a href={`${tree}/repositories/${repoKey}/batteries`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">questions</a> are published with their grading key
    (gold answers, accepted variants, scoring policies and the judge's exemplars). Every arm's
    <a href={`${tree}/runs`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">runs</a> are published in full: the answer to each question, the
    <a href={`${blob}/judging/judge-prompt.md`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">judge</a>'s verdict with its stated reason, the per-run tokens, cost,
    tool calls and time, and the session transcript.
  </p>

  <h2>What every arm could do</h2>
  <p>
    No arm was restricted. Each comparison arm ran in its own container with full permission to call every tool it ships with and any shell
    command, so <code>grep</code>, directory listing and file reading were available to all of them. The codebase-indexing tools were installed
    inside their containers as shipped, and whatever each install adds to the environment (an MCP server, skills, instruction files) was kept;
    the <a href={`${tree}/docker-containers`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">container definitions</a> are published. The TrueArchitect arm had the
    same file tools and one addition: a tool that queries TrueArchitect's index of the codebase. The comparison is therefore between tool sets,
    at the same models, on the same questions.
  </p>

  <h2>Caveats</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Fable 5 refused many questions.</b> The questions concern the structure of a public codebase and touch nothing sensitive, yet the model
      declined a large share of them under the per-question protocols. Repeated attempts did not yield a complete set, so Fable 5 is a thin roster
      in this epoch and its columns should be read with their run counts. The exact figures are on the
      <a href={`${base}/limitations/`}>Limitations</a> page.</li>
    <li><b>Haiku 4.5 and the whole-battery protocol.</b> Haiku 4.5 has a 200K-token context window where the other models have 1M. Under HumanExam,
      which presents the entire battery in one prompt, some Haiku 4.5 runs exceeded it and did not finish. They are published unscored and never enter a mean.</li>
    <li><b>Planned additions.</b> Anthropic's Fable 5.1 and OpenAI's ChatGPT-6 (Astra) will be added in a later epoch.</li>
  </ul>

  <a class="repo-callout" href={REPO_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
    <svg width="28" height="28" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M8 0C3.58 0 0 3.58 0 8c0 3.54 2.29 6.53 5.47 7.59.4.07.55-.17.55-.38 0-.19-.01-.82-.01-1.49-2.01.37-2.53-.49-2.69-.94-.09-.23-.48-.94-.82-1.13-.28-.15-.68-.52-.01-.53.63-.01 1.08.58 1.23.82.72 1.21 1.87.87 2.33.66.07-.52.28-.87.51-1.07-1.78-.2-3.64-.89-3.64-3.95 0-.87.31-1.59.82-2.15-.08-.2-.36-1.02.08-2.12 0 0 .67-.21 2.2.82.64-.18 1.32-.27 2-.27.68 0 1.36.09 2 .27 1.53-1.04 2.2-.82 2.2-.82.44 1.1.16 1.92.08 2.12.51.56.82 1.27.82 2.15 0 3.07-1.87 3.75-3.65 3.95.29.25.54.73.54 1.48 0 1.07-.01 1.93-.01 2.2 0 .21.15.46.55.38A8.013 8.013 0 0 0 16 8c0-4.42-3.58-8-8-8z"/></svg>
    <span class="rc-body">
      <span class="rc-title">The proof package on GitHub — {REPO_URL.replace('https://github.com/', '')}</span>
      <span class="rc-sub">{runs.length.toLocaleString('en-US')} run directories: every question, every answer, every verdict with the judge's reason, every transcript, and the export that produced them. Every number on this site links into it.</span>
    </span>
    <span class="rc-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
  </a>

  <div class="tiles">
    <div class="tile"><div class="v">{runs.length.toLocaleString('en-US')}</div><div class="l">runs published<br/>{valid.length.toLocaleString('en-US')} valid and scored · {dnf} did not finish</div></div>
    <div class="tile"><div class="v">{arms.length}</div><div class="l">arms<br/>1 TrueArchitect · {arms.length - 1} comparison</div></div>
    <div class="tile"><div class="v">{models.length}</div><div class="l">models<br/>Anthropic, OpenAI, xAI, Cursor</div></div>
    <div class="tile"><div class="v">{verdicts.toLocaleString('en-US')}</div><div class="l">question verdicts<br/>one scorer era: {manifest.scorer_eras.join(', ')}</div></div>
  </div>

  <div class="key">
    <h3>Key results — ZeroShot and MultiTurn protocols, both batteries ({manifest.epoch.id})</h3>
    <ol>
      {ratioMin != null && ratioMax != null && (
        <li>
          <b>Context.</b> At every model both arms ran, bare Claude Code read {ratioMin.toFixed(1)}× to {ratioMax.toFixed(1)}× the context tokens
          TrueArchitect read to answer the same battery
          ({pairs.map(p => `${p.model}: ${fmt(ctxFig, p.ta)} vs ${fmt(ctxFig, p.cc)}`).join('; ')}). — <a href={`${base}/results/context-tokens/`}>Figure 1</a>
        </li>
      )}
      {accTA != null && accCC != null && (
        <li>
          <b>Accuracy.</b> TrueArchitect's pooled accuracy is {accTA.toFixed(1)}% against {accCC.toFixed(1)}% for bare Claude Code
          {bestIndexer?.pooled != null ? ` and ${bestIndexer.pooled.toFixed(1)}% for the best indexing tool (${bestIndexer.arm.short})` : ''}. — <a href={`${base}/results/accuracy/`}>Figure 2</a>
        </li>
      )}
      {relTA != null && relCC != null && (
        <li>
          <b>Reliability.</b> {relTA.toFixed(0)}% of TrueArchitect's runs scored at least 90%, against {relCC.toFixed(0)}% for bare Claude Code. — <a href={`${base}/results/pass-rate/`}>Figure 3</a>
        </li>
      )}
    </ol>
    <p class="muted" style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px">Pooled figures average protocol × battery × model cells with equal weight; see each figure's <em>How this figure is computed</em>. Change protocols, battery and model on every figure page. HumanExam (the whole battery in one prompt) is off by default because it is the stress probe, not the usage shape; switch it on to see it.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Headline figure</h2>
  </div>
  <FigureIsland client:load fig={ctxFig} data={packRows(runs, 'ctx')} compact />
  <div class="prose">
  <p class="caption"><b>Figure 1.</b> {ctxFig.caption[0]} {ctxFig.caption[1]} {ctxFig.caption[2]} <a href={`${base}/results/context-tokens/`}>Controls, data table and method →</a></p>

  <h2>The figures</h2>
  <div class="fig-grid">
    {FIGURES.map(f => (
      <a class="fig-link" href={`${base}/results/${f.slug}/`}><span class="n">Figure {f.number}</span><span class="t">{f.title}</span><span class="muted" style="font-size:12px">{f.caption[2]}</span></a>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2>How to read this site</h2>
  <p>An <b>arm</b> is one configuration of tooling; a <b>model</b> is the LLM behind it; a <b>run</b> is one repetition of one arm × model × exam protocol × battery. Runs are independent sessions. Means are taken over valid, scored runs only; a run that did not finish is published unscored and never enters a mean.</p>
  <p>Every figure page has three parts: the figure with its controls, the formal definition of what was computed, and the data table with a link per row into the repository. The <a href={`${base}/method/`}>Method</a> page is the repository's README rendered; the <a href={`${base}/limitations/`}>Limitations</a> page collects every disclosure in one place.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
